fragment_downloaded_cb({"url":"Reference-manual_builtin_meson.html#mesonoverride_find_program","fragment":"meson.override_find_program()\nspecifies that whenever find_program() is used to find a program\nnamed progname, Meson should not look it up on the system but\ninstead return program, which may either be the result of\nfind_program(), configure_file() or executable().\n(since 0.55.0) If a version\ncheck is passed to find_program() for a program that has been overridden with\nan executable, the current project version is used.\n(since 1.12.0) A program can be overridden both for the host machine and for the\nbuild machine.\nUsing override_find_program when cross compiling requires some care.  In particular:\na program built for the host machine should only be registered if the host\nbinaries can be run, otherwise it cannot be executed.\nsince 1.13.0 Meson allows overriding the same program twice if both calls refer\nto the same executable, but this may not work when cross compiling if a\nsubproject is invoked for both the host and build machine.\nmeson.can_run_host_binaries() and meson.is_cross_build() make it possible\nto override the program for both cases:\nSee find_program() for how to use an overridden program.\n\n\n\n\na program built for the host machine should only be registered if the host\nbinaries can be run, otherwise it cannot be executed.\n\n\nsince 1.13.0 Meson allows overriding the same program twice if both calls refer\nto the same executable, but this may not work when cross compiling if a\nsubproject is invoked for both the host and build machine.\n\n\n"});